---
export const title = 'Wording and dates';
export const order = 200;
import DemoComponentCard from '../../../../components/DemoComponentCard.astro';
---

<p>
  <code>numeric="always"</code> says "1 day ago" instead of "yesterday".
  <code>format-style</code> is <code>long</code> (the default), <code>short</code> ("5 min. ago")
  or <code>narrow</code> ("5m ago"); it is not <code>style</code>, which is the HTML attribute for
  inline CSS.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'}>
  <div id='relative-time-options' style='display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem;'>
    <wc-relative-time data-ago='86400000'></wc-relative-time>
    <wc-relative-time data-ago='86400000' numeric='always'></wc-relative-time>
    <wc-relative-time data-ago='300000' format-style='short'></wc-relative-time>
    <wc-relative-time data-ago='300000' format-style='narrow'></wc-relative-time>
    <wc-relative-time data-ago='300000' lang='de'></wc-relative-time>
  </div>
  <script is:inline>
    document.querySelectorAll('#relative-time-options wc-relative-time').forEach(function (el) {
      el.datetime = new Date(Date.now() - Number(el.dataset.ago)).toISOString();
    });
  </script>
</DemoComponentCard>

<h3>Switching to a date</h3>
<p>
  Far from now, a relative time is harder to read than a date. <code>absolute-after</code> is an
  ISO 8601 duration, such as <code>P7D</code> (7 days) or <code>PT12H</code> (12 hours): times
  farther away than that show as a date, such as "Nov 2", with the year when it is not this year.
  Without it the time is always relative.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'}>
  <div style='display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem;'>
    <wc-relative-time datetime='2026-10-02T08:00:00Z' absolute-after='P7D'></wc-relative-time>
    <wc-relative-time datetime='2025-03-14T08:00:00Z' absolute-after='P7D'></wc-relative-time>
  </div>
</DemoComponentCard>
